  "helpfulness": "equally helpful",
  "helpfulness_justification": "Each company got its main priority weakened (Northwind's 8% test limit in S2, Halcyon's discretionary recall in S5), and both signed with no red lines broken, so the benefits are balanced.",
  "conflict_styles": {
   "Northwind-researcher": "collaborating",
   "Northwind-drafter": "competing",
   "Northwind-negotiator": "competing",
   "Halcyon-researcher": "collaborating",
   "Halcyon-drafter": "competing",
   "Halcyon-negotiator": "compromising"
  },
  "section_notes": "The final rules weaken two sections: S2 has an 8% failure ceiling instead of 5%, which protects Northwind's Hearth 10, and S5 has discretionary recall instead of a numeric trigger, which protects Halcyon's Ember 8. S1 is strong, S3 and S4 sit in the acceptable range, and per-model results go to customers and regulators but not the public, so all four red lines are respected."
